---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { displayDateFor } from '../../data/blogs';

const title = 'Best time to sell a used car in India: the month-by-month price index';
const description = 'March and October pay for completely different reasons. The June-August trough is partly an inspection problem. Full 12-month price index, segment-wise best and worst months, and why Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru, Chennai and Kochi peak on different calendars.';
const publishedOn = displayDateFor('best-time-to-sell-used-car-india');
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="container-tight max-w-3xl">
      <div class="mb-8">
        <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-slate-soft mb-3">
          <a href="/blogs">← All blogs</a>
        </div>
        <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-signal-600 font-semibold mb-3">Selling · Timing</div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-serif text-navy-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
          Best time to sell a used car in India: the month-by-month price index
        </h1>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-soft">
          {publishedOn && (
            <>
              <time datetime={publishedOn.iso}>{publishedOn.label}</time>
              <span class="mx-2">·</span>
            </>
          )}
          <span>13 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose-content space-y-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-graphite">

        <div class="card-institutional bg-cream-100 border-l-4 border-signal-600 text-sm text-graphite">
          <strong class="text-navy-900">Framework, not a valuation quote.</strong> The index values and month-by-month behaviours below describe observed patterns across TheResaleValue transaction data. Actual monthly variance depends on model, city, condition and current market conditions. Use the framework as a timing lens, not as a guaranteed premium.
        </div>

        <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
          Used-car offers in India tend to run highest in the six weeks before 31 March, then again across the Navratri-to-Dhanteras stretch in October and November. The floor sits between mid-June and late August. What is easy to miss is that the two peaks share almost no mechanics: March runs on dealer inventory books and business-buyer purchase timing before the financial year closes, October on festive retail demand from individual buyers. Both windows are short. Neither is a "season".
        </p>

        <p>The part most sellers get wrong is the size of the prize. The gap between the best month and the worst month is real but modest, and once depreciation, insurance and any EMI on a waiting car are subtracted, a long wait for the peak often ends in a net loss. Timing is a tiebreaker, not a strategy.</p>

        <p>Before applying any of this to a specific car, run the registration number and kilometres through the <a href="/valuation">free used car valuation calculator</a> so the seasonal adjustment sits on top of a real base number rather than a guess.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Key findings</h2>

        <p>The peak-to-trough spread across the calendar year sits at roughly 6 to 7 index points, meaning a ₹6,00,000 car swings by about ₹36,000 to ₹42,000 on timing alone.</p>

        <p>Two windows totalling roughly eleven weeks hold a majority of the annual price variance: mid-February to 31 March, and Navratri to Dhanteras.</p>

        <p>The remaining forty weeks of the year sit inside a band of about 3 index points, which is often narrower than one round of negotiation.</p>

        <p>Wait eight months for the March peak and the car typically loses more to depreciation than the peak pays, at prevailing depreciation rates of roughly 1% to 1.5% per month in years three to six.</p>

        <p>Chennai inverts the national pattern: its weakest stretch runs October to December during the north-east monsoon, exactly when the rest of India peaks.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">When to sell in India: the peak is a six-week window, not a season</h2>

        <p>Price movement across the Indian used-car year is concentrated, not evenly spread. Most of the annual variance sits in about ten to twelve weeks. For the rest of the year, month-to-month movement is small enough that condition, service history and the buyer's mood matter more than the date on the calendar.</p>

        <p>That distinction matters because "festive season" gets used loosely. Offers do not lift for the whole of September through December. They lift for a date-locked stretch of about four weeks and then fall away.</p>

        <p>One definition has to be nailed down before any index means anything. The series used here is the dealer buying offer, the number a buying desk or organised platform commits to after inspection, not the asking price on a listing site and not the transacted private-sale price. All three move on different schedules, and the difference between them is often larger than the seasonal swing itself. That relationship is covered separately in <a href="/blogs/fair-market-value-vs-asking-price-vs-dealer-offer">fair market value vs asking price vs dealer offer</a>.</p>

        <p>Scale sets the practical answer. A 6% swing on a ₹6 lakh Hyundai Creta is ₹36,000, which justifies planning. That same 6% on a ₹2.4 lakh 2014 Maruti Suzuki Wagon R comes to about ₹14,000, and a seller can lose that in one soft negotiation round regardless of the month.</p>

        <p>Timing also cannot repair anything structural. An odometer past 1,20,000 km, a repaired accident with a replaced pillar, a two-year service gap, or a registration approaching its 15-year mark will price the car below the segment median in March exactly as it will in July. Those sit in <a href="/blogs/factors-affecting-used-car-resale-value">the 10 factors that decide used car resale value</a>, and none of them are seasonal.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Why March is the peak: fiscal-year-end mechanics</h2>

        <p>March is the stronger of the two peaks for most segments because three separate pressures land in the same fortnight: dealer inventory books closing on 31 March, business buyers timing purchases to the financial year, and manufacturer exchange bonuses clearing calendar-year stock. All three raise the number a buying desk will commit to. None of them involve a single individual buyer wanting a car for a festival.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Dealer inventory books close on 31 March</h3>

        <p>Used-car dealers and organised platforms run to the Indian financial year, April to March. Stock physically sitting on the yard on 31 March is stock carried on the books into the new year. Stock sold before that date is not.</p>

        <p>So a short period opens where buying desks are doing two things at once: discounting older inventory to move it out, and restocking to open the new year against fresh targets. A seller walking in during that fortnight is talking to a desk with a live incentive to say yes. Offers come back faster, and the gap between the first quote and the final number tends to narrow.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Business buyers timing purchases before 31 March</h3>

        <p>Businesses, professionals and small fleets buy vehicles near year-end for accounting reasons. A car bought and put to use before 31 March allows depreciation to be claimed against that financial year rather than the next one, which pulls purchase decisions forward into February and March.</p>

        <p>The rule that shapes exactly when in the year they buy is the 180-day test. Under the Income Tax Act, if an asset is put to use for fewer than 180 days in the financial year, only half the normal depreciation rate is allowed for that year. Buying in September or earlier gets the full rate; buying in March gets half. A half-year claim still beats no claim, which is why the March window stays busy even though the rate is reduced. Specific rates and enhanced-rate provisions applicable to motor cars in a given year should be confirmed against the current Income Tax Act position before a purchase decision.</p>

        <p>This demand is not spread evenly. It concentrates in commercial-friendly hatchbacks, diesel MPVs and entry SUVs. A Maruti Suzuki Ertiga diesel, a Toyota Innova Crysta or a Mahindra Bolero sees a sharper March lift than a two-door-mentality premium hatchback.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Exchange offers pull trade-ins into March</h3>

        <p>Manufacturers and dealerships push year-end discounts and exchange bonuses through March to clear stock carrying the previous calendar year's build. For a seller, that creates a second bidder. An exchange quote from a new-car dealership, even one the seller has no intention of accepting, is a usable negotiating instrument against an independent buying desk.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">The March deadline that catches sellers out: paperwork, not price</h3>

        <p>The premium only exists if the transaction actually closes. Money received on 29 March with the ownership transfer filed sometime in April is fine for the seller's bank balance and bad for the seller's liability position, because the car still sits against their name on the VAHAN record until the transfer is processed.</p>

        <p>Transfer of ownership is applied for using Form 29 (notice of transfer, signed by the seller) and Form 30 (application for intimation and transfer, signed by both parties), submitted to the RTO where the vehicle is registered, along with the original RC, insurance, PUC and a valid address proof. The seller retains the stamped acknowledgement. The transfer is complete when the buyer's name reflects on the Parivahan/VAHAN record, not when the cash lands. Current form numbers, fee structure and intimation timelines should be confirmed on parivahan.gov.in before filing.</p>

        <p>That changes the timeline. Late March is the busiest counter period of the year at most RTOs.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Car prices during festive season: what Navratri to Dhanteras does to offers</h2>

        <p>The festive peak works on the opposite side of the market from March. It is a retail demand event, so the extra money comes from individual end-buyers rather than dealer buying desks. That matters for the seller's route choice: individual buyers typically pay closer to fair market value than an instant-buy desk does, because the desk has to price in reconditioning, warranty and its own margin. The trade-offs between those channels are set out in <a href="/blogs/private-vs-dealer-vs-instant-buy">private sale vs dealer vs instant-buy routes</a>.</p>

        <p>Demand in this window is date-locked, and that is where the pricing power sits. Buyers want possession on a specific auspicious date: Dussehra, Dhanteras, or the day before Diwali. A seller who can hand over keys, RC, insurance papers and a signed Form 29 on the required date is negotiating from a stronger position than a seller who needs another fortnight to sort a duplicate RC or clear a pending challan.</p>

        <p>There is a second driver most sellers never spot. When a new-car model carries a multi-month waiting period, buyers who need the car for a festive date pivot to a low-kilometre used example of the same model or an adjacent one. A buyer holding a booking slip for a new mid-size SUV with a four-month delivery estimate becomes a serious bidder for a two-year-old example of that exact model in October.</p>

        <p>The drop after the window is faster than the climb into it. Sellers who list in early October, receive a strong offer, hold out for something better, and then find the market thinned by the second week of November are a recurring pattern.</p>

        <p>Regional caveat: this national pattern does not hold in Kerala, Bengal or Chennai, where Onam, Durga Puja and the north-east monsoon sit on different dates. Those are covered in the city section below.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Worst months to sell: June to August is a monsoon inspection problem, not just low demand</h2>

        <p>The demand explanation is the easy half. School admissions and relocations cluster before June, discretionary spending gets deferred once the rains set in, and buyers are reluctant to do test drives, bank visits and RTO runs in heavy weather. Fewer buyers means fewer competing bids.</p>

        <p>The half nobody explains is mechanical. The same car inspected in August fails more inspection points than it does in November, and inspection findings feed straight into the offer sheet. Water does not have to have entered the cabin for the checks to flag.</p>

        <p>The specific checks that flag disproportionately in July and August: damp in the spare wheel well, water-line staining on the boot floor and under the rear seat base, silt in the door drain holes, corrosion on the seat rail bolts, musty odour traced to a wet HVAC evaporator, condensation inside headlamp housings, rust at the sunroof drain outlets, corroded connector pins on underfloor wiring looms, and surface rust on brake discs after a car has stood a week.</p>

        <p>A genuine flood-history car is distinguished from a merely damp one by a different set of indicators: silt above the seat rail line, ECU and airbag module corrosion, tide marks on the seatbelt webbing when pulled fully out. The two carry very different penalties and buyers routinely conflate them.</p>

        <p>The consequence is asymmetric. A car with no water history can still be marked down in July, because proving the absence of history is harder in a wet month than a dry one. The countermeasure is documentation dated before the rains: a third-party inspection report from April or May, dated service records, and photographs of the boot floor and spare wheel well with the trim lifted.</p>

        <p>City severity varies. Buyers in Mumbai, Chennai and parts of Kerala apply a steeper flood discount and ask harder questions about claim history than buyers in Jaipur or Indore. Some of it is verifiable by the buyer: an own-damage claim reduces or resets the No Claim Bonus, so a renewal notice showing 0% NCB on a car whose policy has run several years without a break is a signal worth asking about. What a buyer cannot do is pull another person's claim file directly. The practical check is the previous year's policy document, the renewal quote showing the NCB slab, and the insurer's confirmation to the policyholder.</p>

        <p>One genuine exception runs the other way. Sellers of high-ground-clearance SUVs and 4x4s in flood-prone metros sometimes see firmer monsoon interest, because the buyer's motivation is the weather itself.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Should you wait to sell? The holding-cost test</h2>

        <p>A car does not stand still while it waits for a better month. Every month of holding adds depreciation, another month of insurance cover, any parking or society charge, any service that falls due, and, if the car is financed, another EMI paid against an asset that is losing value faster than the loan is shrinking.</p>

        <p>The test is arithmetic, not judgement:</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr class="border-b-2 border-navy-900 text-navy-900 text-left">
                <th class="py-2 pr-3">Line</th>
                <th class="py-2">What it is</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-graphite">
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Expected seasonal gain</td><td class="py-2">Current value × peak premium in index points</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Less depreciation over the wait</td><td class="py-2">Current value × monthly depreciation rate × months waited</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Less holding costs</td><td class="py-2">Insurance pro-rata + parking + any scheduled service falling due</td></tr>
              <tr class="border-b border-cream-200"><td class="py-2 pr-3">Less interest cost</td><td class="py-2">Interest portion of EMIs paid during the wait</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="py-2 pr-3">= Net</td><td class="py-2">Positive means wait. Negative means sell now.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>Most of these calculations turn on the monthly depreciation rate, and that rate is not flat across a car's life. The curve by year is in <a href="/blogs/new-car-depreciation-india">how fast a car depreciates each year in India</a>.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Worked example 1, long wait, negative outcome.</strong> A 2018 Maruti Suzuki Swift VXi petrol, around 62,000 km, Chennai. Current value in July, roughly ₹3,80,000. The seller considers holding eight months to the March peak. Seasonal gain at a 6-point spread: about ₹23,000. Depreciation over eight months at 1.2% a month: about ₹35,000. Insurance and parking for eight months: about ₹12,000. The car also crosses a whole-year age band during the wait. Net position: negative by roughly ₹24,000 before counting a single service bill.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Worked example 2, short wait, positive outcome.</strong> A 2022 Kia Seltos HTX, around 29,000 km, Gurugram. Current value in late August, roughly ₹13,50,000. The seller considers holding six weeks to the Dhanteras window. Seasonal gain at a 3-point lift from the August base: about ₹40,000. Depreciation over six weeks at 1.1% a month: about ₹22,000. Insurance and parking: about ₹2,500. No loan outstanding. Net position: positive by roughly ₹15,000, plus a wider pool of individual buyers competing.</p>

        <p>The rule that falls out of the arithmetic: waiting pays when the wait is under about two months and the car sits in a higher-value segment; waiting loses when the wait runs beyond a quarter, when the car crosses an age or kilometre band during the wait, or when a registration or scrappage deadline sits inside the waiting period.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Monthly used car price index for India: the 12-month view</h2>

        <p>The index below is built on dealer buying offers, indexed so that the annual average equals 100. A reading of 103 means offers that month run about 3% above the year's average for the same car in the same condition.</p>

