        <p>Three divergences are worth calling out. CNG-fitted cars respond more to CNG price movement and city refuelling infrastructure than to any month; a price revision moves those valuations more than Diwali does. Diesel demand in NCR is deadline-driven rather than seasonal, because the registration clock overrides everything else. Entry hatchbacks show the narrowest spread of any segment, because that buyer is budget-constrained rather than discretionary and buys when the money is available, not when the calendar suits.</p>

        <p>Applying an index by hand tends to compound error. Run the specific car through the <a href="/valuation">used car value calculator</a> and treat the seasonal adjustment as the last step, not the first.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Best month to sell by city: why the calendar shifts across metros</h2>

        <p>National seasonality is a starting assumption. In five metros it gets overridden by something local, and in one of them the national peak month is the local trough.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delhi NCR: a deadline market more than a seasonal one</h3>

        <p>NCR selling is dominated by registration age, not by the month. Court and tribunal directions have restricted the operation of diesel vehicles beyond 10 years and petrol vehicles beyond 15 years in the region, and enforcement of fuel-supply denial to such vehicles has been revised more than once. The current position should be checked against the Delhi Transport Department and the relevant tribunal directions before pricing.</p>

        <p>The practical effect: a diesel car approaching its tenth registration year loses value on a monthly clock that no festive window compensates for. The alternative route is an NOC from the Delhi RTO and re-registration in another state, which itself takes weeks and carries road-tax consequences. Sellers in this position should read <a href="/blogs/car-scrappage-value-india">vehicle scrappage rules and what a scrapped car pays</a> before waiting for any month.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mumbai: the monsoon discount starts earlier and lasts longer</h3>

        <p>Mumbai's trough opens with the first heavy spells in early June and recovers later than the national pattern, often not fully until October. The flood question gets asked more aggressively here, and asked with specifics rather than generally.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bengaluru: the appraisal-and-relocation calendar</h3>

        <p>Selling in Bengaluru clusters around IT appraisal payouts and inter-city relocation windows more than around festivals. A second factor shapes timing: a large share of cars in the city carry another state's registration, bought by owners who moved for work.</p>

        <p>Selling such a car locally means the buyer will want the registration moved. That requires an NOC from the RTO where the car was originally registered, re-registration in Karnataka, and settlement of the road-tax position in the new state, with a refund claim available in the old one. The NOC alone can add several weeks. Current procedure and refund mechanics should be confirmed against parivahan.gov.in and the Karnataka Transport Department site.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Chennai: the north-east monsoon pushes the trough to October, December</h3>

        <p>This is the clearest exception to "October is the best month". Chennai's heaviest rain arrives with the north-east monsoon from mid-October into December, and flood memory from previous years makes buyers unusually cautious in exactly the weeks when the rest of the country is paying a festive premium. The strong local window sits earlier, in the dry stretch from January to March.</p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-navy-900 mt-8 mb-3">Kochi and Kerala: Onam inverts the monsoon trough</h3>

        <p>Onam falls in August or early September, inside what is nationally the weakest stretch, and it functions as Kerala's main purchase-and-delivery festival. Demand and delivery-date pressure land in a month when the national index is at its floor, which makes late August a genuinely strong local window and the national advice actively wrong for a Kochi seller.</p>

        <p>The local calendar beats the national one every time the two disagree.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Does your car's registration month matter? The December vs January registration gap</h2>

        <p>Yes, but the effect is one of perception, not mechanics. Buyers and pricing systems both think in model years. A car first registered on 28 December reads as a year older from 1 January, four days later, despite being materially identical to a car registered on 3 January.</p>

        <p>Worth checking on the RC before deciding when to list. The consequence is asymmetric: owners of late-December-registered cars generally do better selling in the last quarter of the calendar year than in the first weeks of the next, while owners of January-to-March registered cars have room to hold into the March peak without their car ageing on paper.</p>

        <p>Keep the effect in proportion. It is smaller than the effect of odometer reading, service history or accident repair, and it disappears entirely if the buyer values the car on its manufacturing year instead. The relative weight of age against kilometres is set out in <a href="/blogs/kilometres-vs-age-used-car-resale">kilometres vs age in used car resale</a>.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">Timing the paperwork: insurance renewal, NCB and RC transfer decide your real window</h2>

        <p>The seller's real decision point is not the peak month. It sits four to six weeks earlier, because inspection, negotiation, payment and the ownership transfer all consume time before the money and the liability are both settled.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">Insurance months remaining.</strong> A car handed over with eight months of cover left is a cleaner proposition than one with three weeks left, because the buyer inherits a valid policy and can transfer it rather than arranging fresh cover on a car not yet in their name. Listing in the four weeks before a policy expiry usually means either renewing first or pricing the gap into the offer.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">No Claim Bonus.</strong> NCB is the discount an insurer gives for claim-free years, and it belongs to the policyholder, not to the car. It does not travel to the buyer. A seller who is buying another car can obtain an NCB retention certificate from the existing insurer and apply the accumulated discount to the new vehicle's own-damage premium.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">RC transfer.</strong> Form 29 and Form 30 are submitted to the registering RTO, and the seller's obligation ends when the buyer's name reflects on the VAHAN record under the Motor Vehicles Act, not when payment clears. An open transfer leaves challans, and worse, issued against the seller's name.</p>

        <p>Two common reasons a transfer stalls in practice: an unresolved hypothecation entry where the loan closure NOC was never filed with the RTO, and pending e-challans against the vehicle. The pre-check a seller should run four to six weeks before listing is short: pull the RC status on Parivahan, confirm hypothecation is cleared, and check the challan status on the state e-challan portal.</p>

        <p>Working backwards from the two peaks: a seller targeting the 31 March window should have the car inspected, documented and listed by roughly the third week of February. A seller targeting Dhanteras should be listed by early-to-mid September, before Navratri opens.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">When seasonality does not apply: four seller situations where waiting costs money</h2>

        <p>Seasonal timing is the last variable to optimise, and in these four situations it should be ignored entirely.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">A registration or scrappage deadline inside the waiting period.</strong> The deadline clock beats the calendar, always. A car that crosses its 15-year registration mark in January is not worth holding to March, because the value loss at the threshold dwarfs any seasonal premium. See <a href="/blogs/car-scrappage-value-india">vehicle scrappage rules and what a scrapped car pays</a>.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">A financed car where the outstanding loan is close to the car's value.</strong> Each waiting month worsens the equity position, because the car depreciates faster than the principal reduces in the early-to-middle years of a loan. The comparison is laid out in <a href="/blogs/car-loan-vs-resale-value">whether to keep paying EMIs or sell now</a>.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">A confirmed generation change or discontinuation on the model.</strong> The launch date is the price event, not the festive date. When a new generation arrives, the outgoing generation reprices within weeks regardless of the month.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-navy-900">A condition issue that is actively worsening.</strong> An oil leak progressing from seep to drip. A battery already failing a load test, tyres sitting at the wear indicator, a clutch starting to slip. The deterioration and the inspection markdown that follows it outpace any seasonal premium, and the repair rarely returns its own cost at resale.</p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-serif text-navy-900 mt-12 mb-4">FAQs</h2>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">Is March the best month to sell a car in India?</h3>
            <p>March is one of two annual peaks and usually the stronger one for dealer and business-buyer demand. It runs on financial-year-end inventory clearing and business purchase timing before 31 March, not on festival demand. The premium is only realised if payment and the Form 29/30 transfer complete before the year-end rush congests RTO counters.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">What is the worst month to sell a used car in India?</h3>
            <p>Nationally, the weakest stretch runs from mid-June to late August, with July and August typically the floor. Two causes stack: reduced discretionary buying during monsoon, and a higher rate of adverse inspection findings on damp, corrosion and drain-related checks. Chennai is the main exception, where the weakest period runs October to December.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">Do used car prices go up during Diwali in India?</h3>
            <p>Offers typically strengthen across the Navratri-to-Dhanteras window because individual buyers want possession on specific auspicious dates. The extra money comes from end-buyers rather than dealer buying desks, which is why private sales benefit more than instant-buy quotes. The decline after the window closes is faster than the climb into it.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">Should I wait until the festive season to sell my car?</h3>
            <p>Worth it if the wait is roughly six to eight weeks and the car sits in a higher-value segment. Usually not worth it beyond a quarter. The arithmetic: seasonal gain equals current value times the peak premium; the cost equals monthly depreciation times months waited, plus insurance, plus any EMI interest. If the second number is larger, sell now.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">Do used car prices drop during monsoon in India?</h3>
            <p>Yes, and part of the drop is mechanical rather than demand-driven. Monsoon-period inspections flag damp in the spare wheel well, silt in door drains and corrosion on seat rail bolts more often than the same inspection run in November. A car with no water history can still be marked down because proving absence is harder in July. A dated pre-monsoon inspection report reduces the discount.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">Does the registration month on my RC affect what I get for my car?</h3>
            <p>It affects perceived age. A car registered in late December reads as a year older from 1 January, days after registration, because buyers and pricing systems work in model years. The effect is real but smaller than kilometres, service history or accident repair. Owners of late-December-registered cars generally do better selling before the calendar turns.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">How many weeks before 31 March should I list my car?</h3>
            <p>Roughly four to six weeks, so around the third week of February. Inspection, negotiation, payment clearance and the RC transfer each take time, and RTO counters get busiest in the final fortnight of March.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-navy-900 text-lg mb-2">Is the best month to sell a car the same in Delhi, Mumbai and Bengaluru?</h3>
            <p>No. Delhi NCR selling is largely deadline-driven by end-of-life registration rules rather than seasonal. Mumbai's monsoon discount starts earlier with the first heavy spells and recovers later than the national pattern. Bengaluru clusters around IT appraisal payouts and relocation windows, with inter-state NOC paperwork adding weeks to the timeline.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="my-12 border-cream-200" />

        <p class="text-lg">The peak-to-trough spread across a used-car year in India is real but modest, and it lives inside two short windows. Everything outside those windows is decided by the car, not the calendar. Before waiting for a better month, run the holding-cost test against a current valuation, then decide against the net number, not the seasonal one. The <a href="/valuation">TheResaleValue calculator</a> gives the base to anchor the arithmetic on.</p>

